{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the fixed-slot equality index at head 2d6811db8ca88f58ddb60e5cfe197679fbd0ce15, tree b1e895b6, for request 121a8913 and review request 854c5cfa. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED.\n\nThe head adds eleven commits to main 4c333072 and changes 28 paths. I read the implementation note first, then the production diff in derive, replay and scope, the kernel and contract text, the tests and the sealed evidence. I reviewed it as a kernel change under section 7 of `docs/kernel.md`. A second, independent read of the same source checked equivalence, maintenance, replay, readiness and bounds. I verified each of its findings in the code before using it here.\n\nThe design is right, and the index answers what the scan would have answered. One defect in the SQLite readiness marker can make the index silently incomplete, and the legacy rebuild needs a cheaper hash read before it runs on the deployed rooms.\n\n## Changes requested\n\n1. The readiness marker does not bind the head it describes.\n   - `applyEntry` calls `writer.finishFixed(definition.digest, entry.at)` after every fold (`packages/derive/src/fold.ts:293`). The marker is `{v:1, definition, at}` (`packages/scope/src/sqlite.ts:193-198`). `ensureFixed` skips the rebuild whenever that marker matches (`sqlite.ts:183`). The guard path never checks readiness (`packages/derive/src/guards.ts:115`).\n   - The head adds the fixed tables with `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` and no Durable Object migration, so `wrangler rollback` to the base stays possible. The base code folds entries without touching `fixed_slot` or `fixed_count`, and it leaves the marker row in place.\n   - Scenario:\n     1. Deploy this head. A Membership scope builds its index, and the marker is set.\n     2. Roll back to the base, and add three members.\n     3. Roll forward. `pinned()` calls `ensureFixed`, which finds the marker ready and skips the rebuild.\n     4. `HANDLE_UNUSED` counts only the rows from before the rollback. An `add-member` that reuses one of the three new handles passes, and the duplicate is admitted. Replay folds from genesis and derives `handle-in-use`, so `dap verify` then reports the scope inconsistent.\n   - Fix: write the scope head (seq and hash) into the marker in `finishFixed`. Have `ensureFixed` rebuild unless the marker's head equals `scope().head`. Add a native case that advances the head and the items without the fixed writes, as the base code would, and then shows that the rebuild runs and the guard counts the new items. Add a control that skips the head comparison and fails the same assertion.\n\n2. The legacy rebuild parses every held entry to read a hash.\n   - `rebuildFixed` (`packages/derive/src/fixed-index.ts:43-53`) calls `writer.all()` and then `history(seq)` for each held position. Through `ownOf` that is `store.stored(seq)` and a `JSON.parse` of the whole entry. On SQLite, `putFixedHash` then only compares the result with the same `entry` row's hash.\n   - The rebuild runs in one transaction on the first request to every existing scope after the deploy, whether or not its definition declares a fixed slot. Room A holds about 34,000 entries. If the rebuild exceeds a Durable Object limit, the transaction rolls back, and every later request repeats it. The note says that this cost is unmeasured.\n   - Fix: on SQLite, read `seq` and `hash` from the `entry` rows without parsing payloads. Record one measured rebuild at about Room A's journal size, or state the bound that keeps it inside a request.\n\nThese changes touch production source outside the documents-only rule, so ruling 31962e1f needs one further full gate at the final head. Gate 1 stays failed, and gate 2 stays a pass for source I.\n\n## Against request 121a8913 and rulings 74286ee7 and 841661d0\n\n1. The index rule. Resolved.\n   - The fast path at `guards.ts:103-117` takes exactly one `equals` between a declared fixed slot of the subject type and a field or a constant, in either order. It skips `of` and `part` forms. Every other `where` keeps the scan.\n   - The counts match the full scan:\n     - `same` is canonical equality, and both paths use `normalValue`.\n     - `slotOf` maps an unset slot and an empty list to `null` on both sides.\n     - Raw mode applies when `own` is absent, as the scan's comparison does.\n     - Normalized keys use the held hash from the `entry` row. A floor deletes that row only after its rekey.\n     - Distinct states and the `except` subject are handled the same way.\n   - Every item change goes through the fold's `now` map. No path deletes items. A failed turn rolls back the fixed rows with the rest of the commit.\n2. Replay. Resolved. `guard-incomplete` is an Unavailable answer, and no journal records it, so every recorded entry was judged on a complete scan. The index reproduces that answer. `View.at()` replays the last absolute write for each item and slot, and `putFixedHash` re-checks the floor.\n3. The witnesses. Resolved. Under `guardScan: 3`, the new derive cases answer complete counts for a field and a constant, and the same guard on a non-fixed slot answers `guard-incomplete`. The `job.name` corpus change follows 74286ee7 item 1. The native room admits member 2,001 and refuses a held handle as `guard-failed` `handle-in-use`, with no `guard-incomplete` and no head change. Under 841661d0, three default `dap verify` runs report Membership, Register and Directory consistent. The `--all` run is a measurement, and it stops at its byte budget.\n4. The two controls each fail an ordinary assertion and restore their source.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The worktree is clean. `git merge-tree --write-tree` onto main 4c333072 gives the head tree b1e895b6.\n- Gate 2 at source I, fe9fefb9 with tree 943ec3b7, passes. Its receipt has SHA-256 a7b12c69, exit 0, and the same head and tree before and after. Source I to the head changes only the note and the manifest.\n- I reran the owning files once each, with no whole suite:\n  - `security-entry-points.test.mjs`: 10 of 10;\n  - `fixed-slot-index.test.ts` and `forms-guards.test.ts`: 10 of 10;\n  - `view.test.ts`: 4 of 4;\n  - `fixed-index.test.ts` and `directory-partition-index.test.ts`: 4 of 4.\n- I read the room case's retained report rather than rerun it. It records 1 passed and 0 failed.\n- The builder worktree stayed clean after the runs.\n- The manifest has SHA-256 cccecab3 and 34,177 bytes. It follows ruling 4e199bd8:\n  - all 247 listed files match by size and SHA-256;\n  - `finalization` and `operator-inputs` match their digests, and each SHA256SUMS reruns clean, with no unlisted file;\n  - the root SHA256SUMS, digest 5e9d5acd, covers all 1,298 other files, with none unlisted and none writable.\n- check-doc-links on an export of the head: 760 links, 0 broken.\n- check-notes from 4c333072: 0 warnings.\n- check-commit-messages from 4c333072: 11 checked, 0 flagged.\n- `git diff --check`: clean.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\n1. `applyEntry` stamps the marker even on a store that was never rebuilt. After item 1, a stale marker no longer matches, so this matters less. A guard-path check of readiness would still make a missed rebuild fail closed.\n2. The SQLite `finishFixed` upsert runs on every entry. It is one primary-key write, and it is worth noting as cost.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe index belongs in derive, because runtime and replay must derive the same guard answer. It adds no entry form, capability or kernel item, and checkpoint bytes are unchanged. The kernel-gate section states the boundary correctly. Approved, subject to item 1.\n\n## Security\n\nThe fast path never trusts a supplied index, and its SQL uses primary keys only. Item 1 is an admission defect: after a rollback and a roll-forward, a uniqueness guard can pass for a value that the scope already holds. Item 2 is an availability risk on the first request after the deploy. Changes requested.\n\n## Simplification\n\nOne general rule covers every fixed slot. It has no membership or handle special case, and it reuses the existing equality and floor rules. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved, subject to item 1.\n- Security: changes requested, items 1 and 2.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks: the index rule, its equivalence to the scan, replay, the witnesses, the controls and gate 2 hold. The evidence verifies under ruling 4e199bd8. Items 1 and 2 need a fix and one further full gate.\n\nA head that makes items 1 and 2 can come back for a short review of the diff.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f07ff3b88941b369be0607c4f9cc37eecfd9f4d8","binding":"assigned","head":"2d6811db8ca88f58ddb60e5cfe197679fbd0ce15","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b0c520a8ef74e8e6042611bc89786e77bd6a5775\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:fcbad7a64c74f9c6fcb5c32883b3f955d73a3ce1\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2fd47b1143e3e4af30e42e245c9250f88dd0f7a0","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}